1. RAID (Redundant Array of Independent Disks)
Why RAID exists

Single disk failure causes data loss.

RAID combines multiple disks to improve:

Performance
Reliability
Capacity
Important RAID Levels
RAID 0 (Striping)

Data split across disks.

Disk1: A C E G
Disk2: B D F H


✅ Fast

❌ No fault tolerance

If one disk dies, all data lost.

RAID 1 (Mirroring)
Disk1: ABCD
Disk2: ABCD


✅ High reliability

❌ 50% storage efficiency

RAID 5

Uses striping + parity.

Disk1: A B P
Disk2: C P D
Disk3: P E F


Can survive 1 disk failure.

Very common.

RAID 6

Like RAID 5 but stores two parity blocks.

Can survive 2 disk failures.

Preferred for large storage systems.

RAID 10

RAID 1 + RAID 0

Mirror Pair 1
Mirror Pair 2

Then striped together.


Excellent performance.

Excellent reliability.

Expensive.

Interview Question

Why is RAID 5 less popular with SSDs?

Because parity calculations add overhead and SSD rebuilds create write amplification.

NetApp Angle

NetApp uses RAID-DP (Double Parity) which is similar to RAID 6 and protects against simultaneous disk failures.

